Python Basics: List vs Tuple
When working with collections in Python, we often use Lists and Tuples. They may look similar, but they have key differences 👇

🔹 List
Mutable → You can modify, add, or remove elements.
Defined using square brackets [ ].

Example:
my_list = [1, 2, 3]
my_list.append(4)  # ✅ Works
print(my_list)  # [1, 2, 3, 4]

🔹 Tuple
Immutable → Once created, you cannot change its elements.
Defined using parentheses ( ).

Example:
my_tuple = (1, 2, 3)
my_tuple.append(4)  # ❌ Error: Tuples don’t support modification

✅ Quick Summary:

List → Flexible, slower, more memory.
Tuple → Fixed, faster, memory-efficient.

👉 Use Lists when your data changes.
👉 Use Tuples when your data is constant.
